DEEP[1]=("<i>Aśru-pūrṇa-ākula-īkṣaṇam</i>, “his eyes troubled and full of tears”. The image is vivid: the world’s greatest archer, weeping in front of two armies. Chapter 2 is the longest teaching chapter after chapter 18, and is often called the Gītā in miniature.",
 "Commentators link the three states named here: <i>kṛpā</i>, pity; <i>aśru</i>, tears; <i>viṣāda</i>, despair. Madhusūdana Sarasvatī calls them the marks of one who has mistaken the body for the self. Sañjaya does not yet call Kṛṣṇa the Lord; that title, <i>śrī bhagavān</i>, comes with the next verse, as if the friend becomes a teacher the moment he opens his mouth. The chapter has four movements: Arjuna’s surrender (2.1–10), the knowledge of the Self, sāṅkhya (2.11–30), the duty of a warrior (2.31–38), and the yoga of action and the person of steady wisdom (2.39–72).",
 "When someone breaks down in front of us, the first gift is presence. Kṛṣṇa does not look away. He turns toward Arjuna.")
DEEP[2]=("<i>Kaśmalam</i>, the word for Arjuna’s state, means a dark faintness, a stain on the mind. Kṛṣṇa asks <i>kutas</i>, from where?, a teacher’s question that invites Arjuna to look at the source of his feeling rather than defend it.",
 "<i>Anārya-juṣṭam</i>, “not fit for a noble person”; <i>asvargyam</i>, “not leading to heaven”; <i>akīrti-karam</i>, “bringing dishonour”. Three reasons, from three angles: character, the next world, and reputation in this one. Kṛṣṇa begins on Arjuna’s own ground, with the values of a kṣatriya, before going deeper. Commentators admire this as good teaching: start where the student stands. The larger argument from the Self comes from 2.11. Rāmānuja notes that Kṛṣṇa calls the state <i>viṣama</i>, “at the wrong moment”: even right feelings can come at the wrong time.",
 "Asking “where is this coming from?” is a powerful question to put to our own moods. Feelings often lose their grip once we trace them.")
DEEP[3]=("<i>Klaibya</i> means impotence or unmanliness in the old idiom; the sense is loss of nerve, not any judgement about gender. <i>Parantapa</i>, “scorcher of foes”, reminds Arjuna of his own reputation.",
 "Vivekananda’s reading of this verse became famous through his lectures: strength, not weakness, is the first message of the Gītā. Commentators also note the tone. Kṛṣṇa’s first words are not comfort but challenge. Some find it harsh; most read it as the firm love of a friend who knows Arjuna can rise. <i>Uttiṣṭha</i>, “stand up”, returns at 2.37, 4.42 and 11.33: the Gītā is a long call to stand up. Arjuna does not obey yet; he needs to be convinced, not commanded. The teaching that follows is Kṛṣṇa’s patient attempt to do just that.",
 "Sometimes what we call kindness is really avoidance, and what feels harsh is real care. A good friend says “get up” when we need to hear it.")
DEEP[4]=("Bhīṣma had brought up Pāṇḍu’s orphaned sons in Hastināpura; in the epic Arjuna later recalls how, as a child, he would climb into his lap and call him “father”, and how Bhīṣma would gently say, “I am your father’s father”. Droṇa had taught Arjuna archery and loved him above all his pupils, even above his own son.",
 "<i>Pūjārhau</i>, “worthy of worship”: an honoured elder’s feet are touched with flowers; Arjuna would have to touch them with arrows. The pun on <i>madhusūdana</i> and <i>arisūdana</i>, “slayer of Madhu” and “slayer of foes”, makes his point: you kill demons and enemies, but these are neither. The Taittirīya Upaniṣad (1.11.2) teaches <i>mātṛ-devo bhava, pitṛ-devo bhava, ācārya-devo bhava</i>: treat mother, father and teacher as gods. Arjuna’s anguish is the anguish of a well-taught student. Kṛṣṇa will not deny this reverence; he will show a duty that stands above it here.",
 "We can respect someone deeply and still have to stand against what they are doing. Doing it without losing respect is a mark of maturity.")
DEEP[5]=("<i>Bhaikṣyam</i>, alms: begging was the proper life of a renunciant or student, not of a warrior-prince. For a kṣatriya to beg was a kind of social death.",
 "<i>Rudhira-pradigdhān</i>, “smeared with blood”: Arjuna imagines every pleasure of victory stained. The word <i>gurūn</i>, teachers or elders, is repeated: <i>gurūn ahatvā</i>, “not killing the teachers”, and <i>gurūn hatvā</i>, “having killed the teachers”. The verse turns on that one word. Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja both take <i>artha-kāmān</i> with the teachers: though they fight out of interest, they are still my gurus. In the Bhīṣma Parva, Bhīṣma and Droṇa themselves say they are bound to Duryodhana by the food they have eaten. Arjuna is ready to excuse them; he cannot excuse himself.",
 "We often forgive others more easily than ourselves. That can be generous, or a way of carrying a weight that is not ours.")
DEEP[6]=("Arjuna admits not knowing <i>kataran no garīyaḥ</i>, “which is weightier for us”: winning or losing. The verse is in the longer triṣṭubh metre, which the Gītā uses for moments of high feeling.",
 "<i>Yān eva hatvā na jijīviṣāmaḥ</i>, “those very people, having killed whom we would not want to live”. Commentators see Arjuna’s dilemma sharpen from “I won’t” to “I don’t know”. The shift matters. In chapter 1 he argued for his view. Here he admits that he cannot see the answer. The Upaniṣads prize this moment. In the Kaṭha Upaniṣad, Naciketas will not be put off with riches and asks only for the truth. Arjuna, like Naciketas, sees that no outer gain will do. Śaṅkara calls such a state the doorway to the teaching: only one who knows he does not know can be taught.",
 "Being stuck is uncomfortable, but it is honest. Many good decisions start with “I don’t know which way is right”.")
DEEP[7]=("<i>Śiṣyas te’haṃ śādhi māṃ tvāṃ prapannam</i>, “I am your disciple; teach me, who have taken refuge in you”. This is the moment the Gītā becomes a teaching. In Indian tradition, a teacher teaches when asked; the request is the key.",
 "<i>Dharma-sammūḍha-cetāḥ</i>, “my mind confused about dharma”: Arjuna does not lack courage or skill, but moral clarity. He asks for <i>śreyas</i>, the truly good, <i>niścitam</i>, “with certainty”. Commentators of every school treat this verse as the Gītā’s real beginning. Śaṅkara says the teaching that follows is for removing <i>śoka</i> and <i>moha</i>, grief and delusion. For the Śrī Vaiṣṇava tradition, <i>prapannam</i>, “surrendered”, foreshadows the final teaching of surrender at 18.66: the Gītā begins and ends with refuge. Modern readers see in it the humility every learner needs.",
 "Find someone wise you trust, and be honest with them: “I am confused; help me think.” That one sentence can change the course of a life.")
DEEP[8]=("<i>Ucchoṣaṇam indriyāṇām</i>, “drying up the senses”: grief is pictured as a drought of the spirit. Arjuna names the highest prizes he can imagine: an unrivalled kingdom on earth, and <i>surāṇām ādhipatyam</i>, rule over the gods.",
 "Even Indra’s throne would not cure his grief. Commentators find here the first sign of real dispassion. Unlike 1.32, it is no longer just pain at the cost. It is the insight that outer things cannot touch an inner wound. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (1.2.12) describes the seeker who, having examined the worlds won by action, sees that the uncreated cannot be gained by the created, and goes to a teacher. Arjuna is doing exactly that. The Gītā will later say that pleasures born of contact are <i>duḥkha-yonayaḥ</i>, wombs of sorrow (5.22).",
 "When something hurts deep inside, buying, winning or achieving rarely helps. It is worth asking what kind of help the wound really needs.")
DEEP[9]=("<i>Na yotsya iti</i>, “I will not fight”: two words in Sanskrit. <i>Tūṣṇīṃ babhūva ha</i>, “and he fell silent”. Sañjaya, speaking to Dhṛtarāṣṭra, reports the most hopeful words the blind king could hear.",
 "Commentators admire the craft of this verse: Arjuna has asked to be taught (2.7), yet still announces his decision (2.9). The mind both surrenders and holds on. Kṛṣṇa does not argue with the decision directly; he answers the confusion beneath it. At 18.73 the Gītā will close the circle: <i>kariṣye vacanaṃ tava</i>, “I will do as you say”. Between “I will not fight” and “I will do as you say” lies the whole teaching. Some note that Arjuna’s silence is a gift too: only one who stops talking can listen.",
 "Even after asking for help, we often cling to our own answer. Notice that in yourself, and give the help a real chance.")
DEEP[10]=("<i>Prahasann iva</i>, “as if smiling”. <i>Senayor ubhayor madhye</i>, “between the two armies”, repeats 1.21 and 1.24: we are back in the same spot, but now the conversation turns inward.",
 "Commentators love this smile. Madhusūdana says it shows that Kṛṣṇa saw the problem as small compared to the remedy he held. Some Vaiṣṇava teachers read it as tenderness: the Lord smiles at his friend as a parent smiles at a frightened child. Others note the gentle irony that follows at once in 2.11: “you speak words of wisdom, yet grieve”. The setting is symbolic too. The Gītā is not taught in a forest hermitage or a temple. It is taught in the middle of life’s hardest moment, between two sides, where most of us actually need it.",
 "Wisdom is most useful where life is hardest, not in quiet retreats. The Gītā is a teaching for the middle of things.")
DEEP[11]=("This verse begins Kṛṣṇa’s teaching proper, which runs, with Arjuna’s questions, to the end of chapter 18. <i>Prajñā-vādān</i>, “words of wisdom”, refers to Arjuna’s arguments about family dharma in chapter 1.",
 "<i>Gatāsūn agatāsūṃś ca</i>, “the departed and the not-departed”, the dead and the living. Why not grieve for the living? Commentators explain: neither the body, which must pass, nor the Self, which cannot die, is a proper object of grief. <i>Paṇḍitāḥ</i>, the wise, are those whose knowledge has become vision. Śaṅkara’s introduction to his commentary takes this verse as the start of the Gītā’s central theme. Rāmānuja calls this section the teaching of the self, laying the ground for the teaching of action and devotion that follows. The famous discussion of the Self, 2.11–30, is the Gītā’s philosophical foundation.",
 "Knowing something and living by it are different. This verse asks gently: does what I know actually change how I feel and act?")
DEEP[12]=("<i>Na tv evāhaṃ jātu nāsam</i>, “never was I not”. Kṛṣṇa includes himself, Arjuna and all the kings on the field. The verse speaks of past and future: the self did not begin with this body and will not end with it.",
 "This is the first of many statements of the self’s eternity in chapter 2. The difference between the schools turns partly on this verse. Rāmānuja says “I, you, these kings” cannot be mere appearance: Kṛṣṇa, the truthful teacher, would not use a misleading plural. Śaṅkara replies that the plural follows ordinary speech about bodies, while the real Self is one. Madhva holds that God, selves and matter are eternally distinct. The Gītā’s words have supported all these readings for a thousand years. What all share is that no one Arjuna loves will truly cease.",
 "Someone we love who has died has not become nothing. Many traditions say the same. The Gītā says it with unusual confidence.")
DEEP[13]=("<i>Dehin</i>, “the embodied one”, is the Gītā’s word for the self in a body. <i>Kaumāram yauvanaṃ jarā</i>, childhood, youth, old age: the three ages of a life.",
 "<i>Dehāntara-prāptiḥ</i>, “gaining another body”, is the first mention of rebirth in the Gītā. The argument moves from the known to the unknown. We have seen the body change completely, cell by cell, and the self remain; why fear one more change? Commentators note that <i>dhīra</i>, steady, comes from a root meaning to hold, or from <i>dhī</i>, understanding. The steady one is the one who understands. The theme returns in 2.15, 2.54–72 (the sthita-prajña) and 14.24. Modern readers often meet this verse through its plain logic: if you are not your childhood body, perhaps you are not this body either.",
 "When ageing or illness frightens us, it helps to remember how many bodies we have already outgrown. Something in us has stayed the same through all of them.")
DEEP[14]=("<i>Mātrā-sparśāḥ</i>, “contacts with matter” or “sense contacts”, bring <i>śīta-uṣṇa-sukha-duḥkha</i>, cold and heat, pleasure and pain. Kṛṣṇa calls Arjuna both <i>Kaunteya</i> and <i>Bhārata</i>, son of Kuntī and descendant of Bharata: the gentle and the noble side together.",
 "<i>Āgama-apāyinaḥ</i>, “coming and going”; <i>anityāḥ</i>, “impermanent”; <i>tāṃs titikṣasva</i>, “endure them”. The six virtues of Vedānta in Śaṅkara’s Vivekacūḍāmaṇi are calm, self-control, withdrawal, forbearance, faith and focus; forbearance, <i>titikṣā</i>, comes from this verse. The Buddha also taught that sensations arise and pass; the Gītā’s word here is close in spirit. The point is not to stop feeling but to stop being ruled by every feeling. Commentators stress that endurance is a training. It begins with small things, such as heat, cold and hunger, and grows into steadiness in grief and joy.",
 "Practise on small discomforts: a cold shower, a delayed meal, a rude remark. Letting these pass builds the strength for larger storms.")
DEEP[15]=("<i>Puruṣarṣabha</i>, “bull among men”, is one of Kṛṣṇa’s many honour-names for Arjuna. <i>Na vyathayanti</i>, “do not disturb”, from <i>vyath</i>, to waver or trouble.",
 "<i>Sama-duḥkha-sukham</i>, “equal in pain and pleasure”: the first statement of the Gītā’s great ideal of evenness. Commentators note the order: the one who is undisturbed (<i>na vyathayanti</i>), who is wise (<i>dhīra</i>), and who is even (<i>sama</i>). Together these make one fit, <i>kalpate</i>, for <i>amṛtatva</i>, freedom from death. Śaṅkara reads immortality as liberation through knowledge, Rāmānuja as the self’s release from bondage to matter. The ideal is developed in 2.38 (be the same in gain and loss, victory and defeat), 2.48, 6.7–9, 12.18–19 and 14.24–25.",
 "Evenness is learnable. Each time we do not overreact, we strengthen it a little.")
DEEP[16]=("<i>Nāsato vidyate bhāvaḥ, nābhāvo vidyate sataḥ</i>: “of the unreal there is no being; of the real there is no non-being”. This half-line is among the most commented upon in Indian philosophy.",
 "<i>Tattva-darśibhiḥ</i>, “by the seers of truth”: this is not speculation but what the wise have seen. Śaṅkara builds much of Advaita on this verse: whatever changes is not fully real, because the real does not change; only pure awareness is constant. Rāmānuja reads it more simply: the body is perishable (<i>asat</i> in that sense) and the self imperishable (<i>sat</i>). Madhva reads <i>asat</i> as the non-existent and stresses that God and selves are truly real. Gauḍapāda’s Māṇḍūkya Kārikā and later Advaita texts quote the line often. The verse also echoes the Chāndogya Upaniṣad (6.2.1), <i>sad eva somya idam agra āsīt</i>, “in the beginning, dear one, this was Being alone”.",
 "Put your trust in what lasts. Changing things are to be used and enjoyed, but not leaned on with our full weight.")
DEEP[17]=("<i>Avināśi tu tad viddhi</i>, “know that to be indestructible”. <i>Yena sarvam idaṃ tatam</i>, “by which all this is pervaded”. <i>Avyaya</i>, imperishable, literally “not running out”.",
 "Commentators often compare the Self to space: pots break, but the space inside them is untouched. The word <i>tatam</i> returns in 8.22, 9.4 and 11.38, where Kṛṣṇa says all is pervaded by him. Śaṅkara takes 2.17 and 9.4 as the same truth: the Self of Arjuna is the Self of all, which is Brahman. Rāmānuja reads 2.17 as the individual self pervading its body through consciousness, and 9.4 as the Lord pervading all. The verse also says <i>na kaścit kartum arhati</i>, “no one can bring about its destruction”. No weapon, no time, no enemy.",
 "There is something in you that no event can touch. Knowing that does not stop the hard things, but it stops them from defining you.")
DEEP[18]=("<i>Antavanta ime dehāḥ</i>, “these bodies have an end”. Three adjectives describe the embodied one: <i>nitya</i>, eternal; <i>anāśin</i>, indestructible; <i>aprameya</i>, immeasurable, beyond the means of knowing.",
 "<i>Tasmād yudhyasva bhārata</i>: the first “therefore fight” of the Gītā. Commentators stress the logic: since the real cannot die and the body must die, grief cannot be the ground of action. Śaṅkara says the command is not a new duty but permission: Arjuna’s duty already stood, and grief blocked it. Rāmānuja agrees that the verse removes the obstacle. Gandhi, reading the battle as inner, took “fight” to mean the struggle against one’s own weakness. Tilak, in Gītā Rahasya, read it as a call to do one’s worldly duty fearlessly. The verse supports both readings in their contexts.",
 "Fear and grief can freeze us from doing what we know is right. Clarity about what truly matters can unfreeze us.")
DEEP[19]=("<i>Ya enaṃ vetti hantāram</i>, “one who thinks it a killer”; <i>yaś cainaṃ manyate hatam</i>, “and one who thinks it killed”: <i>ubhau tau na vijānītaḥ</i>, “both do not know”.",
 "Kaṭha Upaniṣad 1.2.19 has nearly the same words: <i>hantā cen manyate hantum, hataś cen manyate hatam</i>. The Gītā draws freely on the Upaniṣads; this is one of several verses taken almost whole. Commentators warn that the verse is about the Self, not about bodies or actions. The Gītā holds that actions have consequences (3.9, 4.17, 18.12), and that harming others is the mark of the āsurika nature (16.9). What 2.19 removes is a false identification: Arjuna thinks he will be the killer of Bhīṣma’s Self, and grieves for Bhīṣma as if he will cease. Both thoughts are mistakes about what a person is.",
 "This verse comforts the grieving. It is never a licence to harm. Read it as consolation, not as permission.")
DEEP[20]=("The verse is in triṣṭubh metre, used for weighty statements. It appears nearly word for word in the Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.18). It is recited in many Hindu funeral rites and memorial gatherings.",
 "<i>Na jāyate mriyate vā kadācit</i>, “it is never born, nor does it ever die”. Yāska’s Nirukta lists six changes of every living thing: being born, existing, growing, changing, decaying and perishing. Commentators note that each word of the verse denies one of them. <i>Ajaḥ</i>, unborn; <i>nityaḥ</i>, eternal; <i>śāśvataḥ</i>, everlasting; <i>purāṇaḥ</i>, ancient, which Śaṅkara glosses as “new even though old”. The last line, <i>na hanyate hanyamāne śarīre</i>, “it is not killed when the body is killed”, answers Arjuna’s deepest fear directly. Many people know no other Gītā verse but this one.",
 "If you have lost someone, this verse can be read slowly, aloud. Many families have found comfort in it for centuries.")
DEEP[21]=("<i>Vedāvināśinaṃ nityam</i>, “one who knows it as indestructible, eternal”. <i>Katham sa puruṣaḥ</i>, “how can that person…”: Kṛṣṇa asks a question rather than stating a rule.",
 "<i>Ghātayati hanti kam</i>, “cause to kill, or kill, whom?”. The verse is central to Śaṅkara’s argument that the knower of the Self is free of all sense of agency. For him it shows that the highest knowledge goes with renunciation of action. Rāmānuja disagrees: the knower still acts, but without the false notion that he is the body or that the self of another can be destroyed. Madhva reads it as a reminder that God is the real doer and the self a dependent agent. The verse is also cited in later debates on the ethics of action. It is a statement about the Self, and the Gītā’s ethics of action are taught elsewhere.",
 "When we stop seeing ourselves as the centre of every outcome, we act with less fear and more care.")
DEEP[22]=("<i>Vāsāṃsi jīrṇāni yathā vihāya</i>, “as one casts off worn-out clothes”. The verse is in triṣṭubh. It is perhaps the Gītā’s single best-known image, recited in films, novels and at countless funerals.",
 "<i>Navāni gṛhṇāti naro’parāṇi</i>, “a person takes new ones”. Commentators note a gentle point: the clothes are changed by the wearer, not the other way round. The self is the stable one; bodies are coverings. In 15.8 Kṛṣṇa will add another image: the self carries the senses and mind from body to body as the wind carries scents from flowers. Śaṅkara stresses that the image is meant to remove grief, not to explain rebirth in detail. Rāmānuja reads it alongside 2.13 as proof of the self’s continuity. Some ask why young people die. Teachers answer that “worn out” means the karma for that body is spent, not that the body is old.",
 "We change clothes without sadness. The Gītā hopes we can learn to see bodies, ours and others’, a little more like that.")
DEEP[23]=("<i>Nainaṃ chindanti śastrāṇi</i>, “weapons do not cut it”; <i>nainaṃ dahati pāvakaḥ</i>, “fire does not burn it”; <i>na cainaṃ kledayanty āpaḥ</i>, “water does not wet it”; <i>na śoṣayati mārutaḥ</i>, “wind does not dry it”.",
 "This is one of the most chanted Gītā verses. Commentators note its order: earth (weapons), fire, water, air. Space, the fifth element, is not named, because the Self is compared to space throughout the Upaniṣads. Śaṅkara says the elements act only on things that have parts; the Self has no parts, so they cannot touch it. Madhva adds that the self is not destroyed even in the cosmic dissolution (<i>pralaya</i>, 8.18–19). For Arjuna, surrounded by bows, spears and fire-tipped arrows, the verse is pointed comfort: none of these can touch what Bhīṣma and Droṇa truly are.",
 "Fear of harm is natural. Remembering that the deepest part of us is beyond harm can let us act bravely where it matters.")
DEEP[24]=("<i>Acchedya</i>, uncuttable; <i>adāhya</i>, unburnable; <i>akledya</i>, unwettable; <i>aśoṣya</i>, undryable. Then five positive words: <i>nitya</i>, eternal; <i>sarva-gata</i>, all-pervading; <i>sthāṇu</i>, stable; <i>acala</i>, unmoving; <i>sanātana</i>, everlasting.",
 "<i>Sarva-gata</i>, “gone everywhere”, is read differently by the schools. For Śaṅkara, the Self is literally all-pervading, being Brahman. For Rāmānuja, the individual self is atomic in size but pervades through its consciousness, as a lamp’s light fills a room. Madhva holds a similar view. <i>Sthāṇu</i>, a post, also means Śiva in other contexts. Teachers explain the many words not as decoration but as a meditation aid: each word removes one more wrong idea of what we are, until only the right one remains.",
 "Read the list of words slowly, one at a time, as a meditation. Each one loosens a fear.")
